1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:04,982
Arjun wants one more shop: the busiest
one, right next to a big school.

2
00:00:05,282 --> 00:00:06,738
But the owner, Mr.

3
00:00:06,738 --> 00:00:09,602
Shetty, says no to almost every new brand.

4
00:00:10,152 --> 00:00:12,261
A poster won't change his mind.

5
00:00:12,261 --> 00:00:13,441
A person might.

6
00:00:13,441 --> 00:00:15,180
This is personal selling.

7
00:00:15,830 --> 00:00:20,845
Personal selling is what Arjun does every
day, face to face, with shopkeepers.

8
00:00:21,195 --> 00:00:24,138
An ad talks to thousands
of people at once.

9
00:00:24,138 --> 00:00:26,451
Arjun talks to one, and listens.

10
00:00:26,801 --> 00:00:31,195
That is why it works best for big,
careful buyers, like a shop deciding

11
00:00:31,195 --> 00:00:32,266
what to stock.

12
00:00:32,916 --> 00:00:34,620
So what is personal selling?

13
00:00:34,920 --> 00:00:39,985
Kotler and Armstrong define it as
personal presentation by the firm's sales

14
00:00:39,985 --> 00:00:43,986
force, to make sales and
build customer relationships.

15
00:00:44,336 --> 00:00:45,920
Notice the last part.

16
00:00:45,920 --> 00:00:47,782
It is not just one sale.

17
00:00:47,782 --> 00:00:49,367
It is a relationship.

18
00:00:50,017 --> 00:00:51,961
They also give seven steps.

19
00:00:51,961 --> 00:00:53,854
Let's watch Arjun use them.

20
00:00:54,204 --> 00:00:55,104
One.

21
00:00:54,730 --> 00:00:56,566
Prospecting and qualifying.

22
00:00:56,566 --> 00:01:00,858
Arjun lists twenty shops near
schools, and picks the five best.

23
00:01:01,208 --> 00:01:02,108
Two.

24
00:01:01,717 --> 00:01:02,885
The pre-approach.

25
00:01:02,885 --> 00:01:04,323
He learns about Mr.

26
00:01:04,323 --> 00:01:05,223
Shetty.

27
00:01:04,982 --> 00:01:07,449
His worry is stock that doesn't sell.

28
00:01:07,799 --> 00:01:08,699
Three.

29
00:01:08,393 --> 00:01:09,386
The approach.

30
00:01:09,386 --> 00:01:14,147
Arjun visits at three o'clock, when
the shop is quiet, with a free taste.

31
00:01:14,497 --> 00:01:15,397
Four.

32
00:01:15,064 --> 00:01:16,300
The presentation.

33
00:01:16,300 --> 00:01:21,082
He shows how busy parents buy tiffin
snacks, right here, every morning.

34
00:01:21,432 --> 00:01:22,332
Five.

35
00:01:22,010 --> 00:01:23,423
Handling objections.

36
00:01:23,423 --> 00:01:24,323
Mr.

37
00:01:23,897 --> 00:01:26,549
Shetty asks, what if it doesn't sell?

38
00:01:26,899 --> 00:01:28,751
Arjun says, take two boxes.

39
00:01:28,751 --> 00:01:31,474
We will take back any
packs that don't sell.

40
00:01:31,824 --> 00:01:32,724
Six.

41
00:01:32,294 --> 00:01:33,194
Closing.

42
00:01:32,949 --> 00:01:35,567
Shall I set up the stand by your counter?

43
00:01:35,917 --> 00:01:36,817
Seven.

44
00:01:36,542 --> 00:01:37,442
Follow-up.

45
00:01:37,322 --> 00:01:41,682
Next Monday, Arjun comes back,
counts the packs, and restocks.

46
00:01:42,332 --> 00:01:43,283
Ads talk.

47
00:01:43,283 --> 00:01:44,815
Salespeople listen.

48
00:01:45,515 --> 00:01:47,912
Why use a person, when an ad is cheaper?

49
00:01:48,262 --> 00:01:49,198
Two-way talk.

50
00:01:49,198 --> 00:01:52,816
The buyer can ask questions,
and get answers right away.

51
00:01:53,166 --> 00:01:54,499
A tailored pitch.

52
00:01:54,499 --> 00:01:57,705
Arjun changes his words
to fit each shopkeeper.

53
00:01:58,055 --> 00:01:58,955
And trust.

54
00:01:58,922 --> 00:02:01,224
People buy from people they trust.

55
00:02:01,874 --> 00:02:03,841
Where do we see this at work today?

56
00:02:04,191 --> 00:02:05,109
Prospecting.

57
00:02:05,109 --> 00:02:08,579
A bank's agent lists small
businesses near the branch.

58
00:02:08,929 --> 00:02:09,949
Presentation.

59
00:02:09,949 --> 00:02:13,288
A phone store lets you
try the camera yourself.

60
00:02:13,638 --> 00:02:15,038
Handling objections.

61
00:02:15,038 --> 00:02:18,780
A car showroom offers a test
drive to a doubtful buyer.

62
00:02:19,130 --> 00:02:20,030
Follow-up.

63
00:02:19,968 --> 00:02:23,761
An insurance agent calls
before your policy runs out.

64
00:02:24,411 --> 00:02:26,903
But is personal selling always good?

65
00:02:26,903 --> 00:02:27,855
Not always.

66
00:02:28,205 --> 00:02:29,247
The good side.

67
00:02:29,247 --> 00:02:32,981
Trust grows, and the seller hears
what customers really think.

68
00:02:33,331 --> 00:02:34,479
The hard side.

69
00:02:34,479 --> 00:02:36,155
It is slow and costly.

70
00:02:36,155 --> 00:02:38,553
And some salespeople push too hard.

71
00:02:38,903 --> 00:02:43,367
Arjun is tempted to promise, this
will sell a hundred packs a week.

72
00:02:43,367 --> 00:02:44,443
Zoya says no.

73
00:02:44,443 --> 00:02:46,287
Promise only what is true.

74
00:02:46,637 --> 00:02:50,774
Kotler and Armstrong call personal
selling the most expensive promotion

75
00:02:50,774 --> 00:02:52,586
tool, per person reached.

76
00:02:52,936 --> 00:02:53,836
The lesson?

77
00:02:53,830 --> 00:02:56,073
Sell by helping, not by pushing.

78
00:02:56,723 --> 00:02:57,681
Quick check.

79
00:02:57,681 --> 00:02:58,581
Mr.

80
00:02:58,135 --> 00:03:00,674
Shetty asks, what if it doesn't sell?

81
00:03:00,674 --> 00:03:03,263
Arjun offers to take back unsold packs.

82
00:03:03,263 --> 00:03:04,685
Which step is this?

83
00:03:06,585 --> 00:03:08,076
Handling objections!

84
00:03:08,076 --> 00:03:11,078
Arjun answers the buyer's worry, honestly.

85
00:03:11,678 --> 00:03:12,690
So, remember.

86
00:03:12,990 --> 00:03:17,600
Personal selling is face to face, to
make sales and build relationships.

87
00:03:17,950 --> 00:03:21,148
It has seven steps, from
prospecting to follow-up.

88
00:03:21,498 --> 00:03:24,609
And it works through listening,
a tailored pitch, and trust.

89
00:03:25,209 --> 00:03:29,467
Next time: public relations, and
a newspaper story about Nani.
